'b1-f2b1ebca', 'content': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is an identification applied 
to a gold signet ring—or possibly to one of several gold signet 
rings—associated in modern discussion with the Mycenaean site of Pylos in 
Messenia. The supplied evidence does not establish that name as the formal 
designation of a particular object. A 2016 study by Jack L. Davis discusses 
four gold signet rings bearing Minoan ritual scenes from a Late Helladic IIA 
tomb near the Palace of Nestor, but does not identify any one of them as the 
Ring of Nestor.[3] A separate exhibition listing describes a Minoan gold 
“Signet Ring with a Ritual Scene” dated 1630–1440 BCE and held by the 
Archaeological Museum of Chora, but likewise does not equate it with the Ring 
of Nestor.[5]\n\nThe association with Nestor derives from the archaeological 
setting rather than from a demonstrated ancient connection to the Homeric king.
The Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos was built shortly after 1300 BCE as an 
administrative centre in Messenia and was destroyed by a major fire around 1200
BCE.[2] Because the available evidence does not securely identify the object, 
its exact provenance, catalogue designation, and interpretation as a 
specifically “Nestor” ring remain unconfirmed.[5]', 'evidence_refs': ['e8', 
