[]}, {'chapter_id': 2, 'bundle_token': 'b2-5c57239d', 'content': '## 
Description and archaeological context\n\nThe available material does not 
provide a confirmed catalogue entry under the name “Ring of Nestor,” nor does 
it supply measurements, detailed construction data, or a secure excavation 
record for an object with that name.[5] The closest explicitly documented group
is the set of four gold signet rings reported by Jack L. Davis in connection 
with a large stone-built tomb discovered in May 2015 near Tholos Tomb IV at the
Palace of Nestor, Pylos. The tomb is dated in the supplied abstract to the Late
Helladic IIA period. Its single male occupant, aged approximately 30–35, was 
designated the “Griffin Warrior”; hundreds of objects made of gold, silver, 
bronze, ivory, and semiprecious stone were found with the burial.[3]\n\nDavis’s
study identifies the four gold objects as signet rings decorated with Minoan 
ritual scenes.[3] The supplied material does not provide the individual 
inventory numbers or enough object-level information to determine whether any 
one of these four rings is the object intended by the name Ring of Nestor. It 
therefore would be misleading to transfer the tomb’s date or excavation context
to another ring without an explicit identification.[3]\n\nA separate object 
listing records a gold “Signet Ring with a Ritual Scene,” attributed to the 
Minoan tradition and dated 1630–1440 BCE. The listing assigns it to the 
Archaeological Museum of Chora under museum number SN24-0030 and gives the 
exhibition inventory number VEX.2025.1.81.[5] That entry does not state a 
findspot, excavation context, ownership history, or scholarly publication for 
the ring, and the source does not call it the Ring of Nestor.[5]\n\nThe wider 
Pylos setting is secure independently of the disputed identification. The 
Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos functioned as the administrative centre of a 
kingdom controlling almost the whole of Messenia after its construction shortly
after 1300 BCE; the palace and the hill were abandoned after a major fire 
around 1200 BCE.[2] These dates describe the site’s history and do not 
establish the date or provenance of a particular ring.[2]', 'evidence_refs': 
['e18', 'e20', 'e8', 'e9', 'e10', 'e12', 'e5', 'e6', 'c1', 'c2', 'c5', 'c6'], 
'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 
'b3-7e969d89', 'content': '## Interpretation and reception\n\nThe securely 
documented interpretation concerns the imagery of the four signet rings from 
the Griffin Warrior tomb, not a separately authenticated object called the Ring
of Nestor. They are described as bearing Minoan ritual scenes, indicating that 
the rings were treated as evidence for the circulation or use of Minoan visual 
and religious forms in the Pylos context.[3] One figure caption in the study 
describes a scene in which a male bull-leaper dismounts in mid-air behind a 
bull; the caption also discusses the bull’s tail, horns, hump, and skin folds, 
together with details of the leaper’s clothing and hair.[3] The supplied 
material does not provide a complete iconographic interpretation or prove that 
this particular ring is the Ring of Nestor.[3]\n\nThe name “Ring of Nestor” 
should therefore be distinguished from the archaeological descriptions. 
“Nestor” is securely attached to the palace and kingdom at Pylos in the 
exhibition context: the palace is presented as a Messenian administrative 
centre built shortly after 1300 BCE and destroyed around 1200 BCE.[2] The 
evidence supplied here does not establish that a ring belonged to a historical 
ruler named Nestor, that it was known by that name in antiquity, or that it can
be identified with an object mentioned in Homeric tradition.[2][3]\n\nThe 
alternative exhibition listing reinforces the need for caution. It records a 
Minoan gold signet ring with a ritual scene, a date of 1630–1440 BCE, and Chora
museum and exhibition numbers, but provides no explicit link to the Griffin 
Warrior tomb or to the title Ring of Nestor.[5] The two records may concern 
related material from the Pylos region, but the supplied evidence does not 
demonstrate that they describe the same object. Accordingly, interpretations of
the ring’s imagery can be stated for the documented signet rings, while the 
object identity and the “Nestor” attribution remain unresolved.[3][5]', 
