│                               Ring of Nestor                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The Ring of Nestor is a name associated with a gold signet ring, or with gold  
signet rings, in connection with the Mycenaean site of Pylos in Messenia. Jack 
L. Davis’s 2016 study, The Lord of the Gold Rings, concerns four gold signet   
rings bearing Minoan ritual scenes that were found in a Late Helladic IIA tomb 
near the Palace of Nestor, but it does not identify any of them as the Ring of 
Nestor.[3] An exhibition listing separately describes a Minoan gold “Signet    
Ring with a Ritual Scene,” dated 1630–1440 BCE and assigned to the             
Archaeological Museum of Chora; that listing also does not use the name Ring of
Nestor.[5]                                                                     

The Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos was built shortly after 1300 BCE as an   
administrative centre in Messenia and was destroyed by a major fire around 1200
BCE.[2] The documented records therefore establish a Pylos archaeological      
context for related gold signet rings, but they do not establish that the four 
rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb and the Chora exhibition object are the    
same object, or provide a formal catalogue identification for a ring called the
Ring of Nestor.[3][5]                                                          


                    Description and archaeological context                     


                    Description and archaeological context                     

Davis’s 2016 study records four gold signet rings bearing Minoan ritual scenes 
among the grave goods of a large stone-built tomb discovered in May 2015 near  
Tholos Tomb IV at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos. The study’s abstract dates the  
tomb to the Late Helladic IIA period. It identifies the single male occupant as
approximately 30–35 years old and uses the designation “Griffin Warrior”;      
hundreds of objects made of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious stone
accompanied the burial.[3]                                                     

The study identifies the four gold objects as signet rings decorated with      
Minoan ritual scenes.[3] Its abstract and the publication information          
reproduced with it do not give the individual inventory numbers or state that  
any one of the four rings bears the name Ring of Nestor.[3] The tomb’s date and
excavation context consequently describe those four documented rings and should
not be assigned to another ring without an explicit identification.            

An exhibition preview published by Art of The Ancestors lists a gold “Signet   
Ring with a Ritual Scene,” attributes it to the Minoan tradition, dates it to  
1630–1440 BCE, and assigns it to the Archaeological Museum of Chora under      
museum number SN24-0030 and exhibition number VEX.2025.1.81.[5] The preview    
does not state the object’s findspot, excavation context, ownership history, or
scholarly publication, and it does not call the object the Ring of Nestor.[5]  
These details are therefore exhibition-listing information rather than         
independently corroborated facts about the identity or provenance of the Ring  
of Nestor.                                                                     

The Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos provides the wider archaeological        
setting. The National Archaeological Museum describes the palace as an         
administrative centre of a kingdom controlling almost the whole of Messenia,   
built shortly after 1300 BCE and destroyed by a major fire around 1200 BCE.[2] 
Those dates concern the palace and site history; they do not date or establish 
the provenance of either the four Griffin Warrior rings or the Chora-listed    
object.[2][3][5]                                                               


                         Interpretation and reception                          


                      Iconography of related signet rings                      

Davis’s study describes the four signet rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb as 
bearing Minoan ritual scenes.[3] A caption for one figure describes a male     
bull-leaper dismounting in mid-air behind a bull. It also describes the bull’s 
tail crossing in front of the leaper’s left arm and records details of the bull
and the leaper’s clothing and hair.[3] The supplied description identifies the 
imagery on one of the four rings studied by Davis; it does not identify that   
ring as the Ring of Nestor.[3]                                                 

The Pylos connection is documented through the archaeological setting. The     
National Archaeological Museum presents the Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos  
as a Messenian administrative centre built shortly after 1300 BCE and destroyed
around 1200 BCE.[2] Davis’s study places the four signet rings in a tomb near  
that palace, while the Chora exhibition preview lists a separate Minoan signet 
ring with a ritual scene.[2][3][5]                                             

The two records should be kept distinct. Davis’s publication concerns four     
rings from a specified tomb and reports their Minoan ritual imagery; the Chora 
preview gives a date, material, museum number, and exhibition number for       
another listed ring, but does not link it to the tomb or use the name Ring of  
Nestor.[3][5] On the cited descriptions, the bull-leaping imagery can be       
attributed to one of the Griffin Warrior tomb rings, while the identity of an  
object formally called the Ring of Nestor is not established by these          
records.[3][5]                                                                 


